---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="0.12.0 — Easier sign-in, contributions without an invite"
  description="Trace Commons 0.12.0: Sign in to NEAR AI, contribute without an invite, check session receipts, and import opencode exports."
>
  <p class="release-date"><time datetime="2026-09-09">9 September 2026</time></p>
  <h2>0.12.0 — Easier sign-in, contributions without an invite</h2>

  <p>
    v0.11 let you connect Claude Code and Codex to NEAR AI through the Private AI
    screen. v0.12 makes it easier to use your own NEAR AI account and share
    records of your work with AI.
  </p>

  <p>This release adds four capabilities:</p>

  <ul class="notes">
    <li><strong>Sign in to NEAR AI from the desktop app</strong> with GitHub or Google.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contribute without an invite</strong> when your session includes a
      receipt that Trace Commons can check.
    </li>
    <li><strong>See which sessions have receipts</strong> and which you can submit.</li>
    <li><strong>Import sessions exported from opencode.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sign in and connect your tools</h2>

  <p>
    Open Private AI on macOS, Windows or Linux and choose sign-in. Your browser
    opens NEAR AI, where you sign in with GitHub or Google. The app then saves an
    API key on your computer. This key lets your coding tools use your NEAR AI
    account.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can connect Claude Code or Codex once a key is available. If Private AI is
    already running, it picks up the new key without a restart. You can also
    cancel a sign-in from the app.
  </p>

  <p>
    The account card shows your spending, your spending limit if you have one, and
    when the balance was last checked. If your account has no limit, the card says
    so. It also tells you when requests use a key set up outside the app.
  </p>

  <p>
    Using NEAR AI and sharing sessions remain separate choices. You choose whether
    to join as a contributor and which sessions to share.
  </p>

  <h2>Contribute without an invite</h2>

  <p>
    A trace is a record of a session with an AI tool. In v0.12, you can join Trace
    Commons with your NEAR AI login and contribute traces that include checkable
    receipts.
  </p>

  <p>
    A receipt is a digitally signed record of an AI request and its response.
    Trace Commons checks the signature and confirms that the receipt matches the
    content being submitted. This lets us accept those contributions without
    someone first sending you an invite.
  </p>

  <p>
    Receipts can come from models NEAR AI runs itself or from its service that
    forwards requests to providers such as Anthropic, OpenAI and Google. Trace
    Commons accepts both. Their limits are explained below.
  </p>

  <h2>See what you can share</h2>

  <p>The session list now helps you answer two questions:</p>

  <ul class="notes">
    <li>
      <strong>Does this session have a checkable receipt?</strong> Everyone sees
      this status, including contributors who joined with an invite.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Can I submit this session?</strong> If you joined without an invite,
      the app checks whether each session meets the receipt requirement before you
      send it. Contributors with an invite can continue submitting sessions
      without receipts.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Sessions that cannot be submitted stay visible, with a reason. The app
    explains when a recording is incomplete and when changing a setting can help
    future sessions. If the receipt service cannot be reached, the status is
    “unknown”; a later check may succeed.
  </p>

  <p>“Submit all” sends the sessions that can be submitted and reports how many it skipped.</p>

  <h2>Import opencode sessions</h2>

  <p>
    You can now import sessions saved with opencode's export command. Choose an
    import folder and place your exports there for Trace Commons to pick up.
    Imported sessions follow the same contribution rules as other sessions.
  </p>

  <p>
    On macOS, choose the folder in the app. On Windows and Linux, set the folder
    in the contributor service's settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Current limits</h2>

  <ul class="notes">
    <li>
      <strong>Receipts cover the recorded exchange, not every claim about
      it.</strong> A receipt from NEAR AI's forwarding service confirms the
      request and response it handled, but does not prove which model answered.
      For models NEAR AI runs itself, identifying the model still relies on NEAR
      AI's statement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Older sessions without receipts cannot use the invite-free
      path.</strong> Receipts must be captured as part of recording the work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in is available in the desktop apps with GitHub or
      Google.</strong> Wallet sign-in and command-line sign-in are not supported
      yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>opencode support covers exported sessions.</strong> Connecting
      opencode to Private AI is still unavailable. We expected to add it in v0.12,
      but testing showed that the connection could change its selected model.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting an API key removes it from this computer.</strong> To
      disable the key at NEAR AI, revoke it in your NEAR AI account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contributing does not yet earn credit for AI use.</strong> This
      release does not change pricing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Getting it</h2>

  <p>
    The desktop app updates itself. The command-line tool is available through <a
    href="/install/">Homebrew</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    After updating, open Private AI to sign in, or open your session list to see
    which recordings are ready to share.
  </p>
</Base>

